TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "play type distribution",
    "player initiation",
    "teammate role reallocation",
    "minutes played",
    "same-position minutes"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define and measure 'initiation profile'?",
    "How to define 'primary play types' for an absent player?",
    "How to quantify a 'gainer' in play types?",
    "How to identify the 'closest teammate' based on various criteria?",
    "What constitutes a 'player absence' and what duration is relevant?",
    "How to establish baseline (pre-absence) and post-absence periods?",
    "How to control for confounding factors in observed changes?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior project research regarding this specific question or related methodologies."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available, so nothing is established or left open by prior project research."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question proposes a specific hypothesis about which teammate will absorb the absent player's primary play types.",
    "The data available includes play-by-play and derived play-type records, which are suitable for defining initiation profiles and play types.",
    "The primary challenge lies in the limited number of 'cases' (11 unique absent players) which severely constrains generalizability and statistical power, even if there are more 'spells' (148 absence periods).",
    "Multiple criteria are suggested for identifying the 'closest teammate', requiring a clear methodology for selection or comparison."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The exact definition of 'initiation profile' and how 'closeness' will be quantified across different metrics (initiation profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes) is not specified.",
    "The duration threshold for a 'player absence' spell is not defined.",
    "How to control for natural variation in player roles, team strategy changes, and other simultaneous player absences or returns is unclear.",
    "Whether 'primary types' refers to specific play types or a broader category needs clarification."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available from previous project research, so the decision was not influenced by prior findings."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.1,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "With only 11 unique absent player 'cases' and 148 'spells', it is highly unlikely to achieve sufficient statistical power to robustly test the hypothesis or generalize findings, especially if the effect is subtle or highly variable across players/teams. The question implies identifying *the* largest gainer, which requires sufficient distinct events."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Many factors can confound offensive reorganization during player absences, such as other player injuries/returns, coaching decisions, opponent defensive schemes, game context, and natural team evolution. Without strong controls or a larger sample, isolating the specific effect of 'closest teammate' will be difficult."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No historical evidence was available from prior project research to indicate whether this question has been answered."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The small number of 'cases' (11 unique absent players) and 'spells' (148 absence periods) may limit statistical power and generalizability. Detecting a consistent pattern across such a small and potentially diverse set of absences is challenging."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Changes in offensive roles and play-type distribution can be influenced by numerous factors beyond the 'closest teammate' hypothesis, including other roster changes, coaching adjustments, opponent characteristics, and broader team performance trends."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Some players may have very few 'initiations' or 'primary types' pre-absence, making their 'profile' difficult to establish reliably or making gains by teammates statistically unstable."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The stability and reliability of 'initiation profiles' and 'primary play types' over time, especially across different game contexts, need to be considered. Short pre-absence baselines might not be representative."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "Identifying the 'largest gainer' is a relative outcome that can be sensitive to measurement error or arbitrary thresholds for what constitutes a 'gain'. This could also be affected by players who were already trending upwards or downwards."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "The research question offers several criteria for defining the 'closest teammate' (initiation profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes). Testing multiple criteria without adjustment could lead to spurious findings."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.6,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The estimand is the difference in the proportion or volume of the absent player's primary play types initiated by the identified 'closest teammate' during the absence period, compared to their pre-absence baseline, relative to other teammates. Specifically, it seeks to quantify if the designated 'closest teammate' shows the greatest increase in these play types.",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis would be an 'absence spell' (n=148). For each spell, potential 'gaining' teammates would be identified and their changes tracked. Alternatively, a 'spell-teammate' pair could be the unit.",
    "sample": "The available sample consists of 148 absence spells from 11 unique absent players across the NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 seasons. A minimum absence duration (e.g., >3 games missed) and minimum pre-absence play volume for both the absent player and potential gainers would be required to ensure meaningful data points.",
    "power": "Power is expected to be critically low due to the limited number of 'cases' (11 unique players) and the high variability expected across different absence contexts. A formal power calculation would likely indicate the need for a much larger sample to detect a meaningful effect size reliably.",
    "controls": "To mitigate confounding, a difference-in-differences approach could be considered, comparing the change in play-type initiation for the identified 'closest teammate' to other teammates (or a control group of similar teammates not impacted by an absence) during the absence period versus a baseline. Controls for team-level factors (e.g., other simultaneous injuries, coaching changes, opponent strength) and player-specific trends would be essential, possibly using fixed effects for absent players or teams. A synthetic control approach could also be explored if enough similar 'control' situations exist.",
    "falsification": "The null hypothesis is that there is no systematic relationship between a teammate's pre-absence initiation profile/role (based on the defined criteria) and their relative gain in the absent player's primary play types during the absence period. Falsification would occur if the designated 'closest teammate' does not consistently exhibit the largest or a statistically significant gain.",
    "multiplicity": "Given multiple criteria for 'closest teammate' (initiation profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes), a pre-specified hierarchy of criteria or a method for combining them would be necessary. Alternatively, separate analyses for each criterion could be conducted with appropriate multiple testing corrections (e.g., Bonferroni, FDR).",
    "decision_rule": "A statistical significance level (e.g., p < 0.05) would be used. However, due to anticipated low power, a primary decision rule might be based on the consistency and magnitude of the effect across spells, along with sensitivity analyses to assess robustness against confounding factors. If the sample size proves prohibitive, the decision rule might shift to determining feasibility for future research rather than hypothesis testing.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete from 2026-04-13), NBA 2025-26 playoffs (complete from 2026-06-21)."
  }
}

